# SyncSpace Technical Specification

This is the project's architecture and product source of truth. It describes planned behavior explicitly; `process.md` records implementation status. Update this file with every architecture-level change.

## 1. Project Overview

SyncSpace is a multi-user workspace, project, board, and task application. Phases 0 and 1 have established governance and the web/API/infrastructure foundation. Product features are planned, not shipped.

## 2. Product Vision

Users create workspaces, invite colleagues, organize work on boards, and see authorized changes in near real time. The goal is a credible, maintainable collaboration product that demonstrates real engineering tradeoffs.

## 3. Core Product Requirements

Hierarchy: User → Workspace → Project → Board → Column → Task. Workspace membership and token invitations are implemented; project/board/task collaboration, assignees, comments, labels, attachments, watchers, activity, notifications, authenticated sockets, presence, optimistic UI, and conflict detection remain planned. MVP is the two-user journey defined in section 38.

## 4. Non-Goals

No microservices, Kafka, hand-built CRDT, permanent presence records, or large analytics system in the MVP. Mobile native apps are outside the initial scope.

## 5. Mandatory Engineering Rules

Read `AGENTS.md`, this file, and `process.md` before changing code. Every meaningful commit updates `process.md`; architecture changes update this file. Never store secrets, trust client-supplied identity/role, skip server authorization, or claim tests passed without running them. UTC storage, UUID resource IDs, migrations for schema changes, and explicit documentation of debt are required.

## 6. Technology Stack

pnpm monorepo; Next.js App Router, React, strict TypeScript, Tailwind CSS; NestJS API; PostgreSQL with Prisma migrations; Redis; Supabase Auth with `@supabase/ssr` and JOSE JWT verification. Socket.IO enters Phase 6. Dependencies are pinned in the lockfile. TanStack Query, React Hook Form, and dnd-kit enter when their features are implemented.

## 7. System Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
 B[Browser] --> W[Next.js]
 W -->|REST authoritative| A[NestJS]
 W -. Socket.IO events .-> A
 A --> P[(PostgreSQL)]
 A --> R[(Redis)]
```

The browser sends resource mutations through REST. The API validates identity, membership, input, and version, commits the database transaction, then publishes a small event. Clients reconcile against canonical REST data. Socket delivery is best effort; reconnects reauthenticate and refetch. Phase 1 provides only the health path and connectivity checks.

## 8. Repository Structure

`apps/web` Next.js, Supabase SSR helpers, and workspace/project/board pages; `apps/api` NestJS, Prisma, auth, users, workspaces, and projects; `packages/shared` public contracts when introduced; `scripts` governance; `docs` supplemental reviews/operations notes; root config and governance files. Do not share Prisma models with browser code.

## 9. Frontend Architecture

Use App Router and server rendering where practical. Auth pages use a Supabase browser client; protected `/app` pages use a server client with cookie sessions, `getClaims()` verification, and a bearer token to fetch canonical API data. Middleware refreshes cookies and forwards private cache headers. Invite continuation is restricted to `/invite/<token>` and stored briefly in an HttpOnly cookie through OAuth. Client components handle interactive forms and sign-out. Phase 3 pages list/create workspaces, manage members/settings, show a one-time invitation link, and accept invitations. Phase 4 pages list/create projects and boards, edit project settings, and add/rename/reorder/delete columns with version conflict feedback. TanStack Query will own remote cache and socket-triggered invalidation later; lightweight local state owns view-only state. Board drag and drop will have keyboard alternatives. Theme modes: light, dark, system, persisted per user in a later phase.

## 10. Backend Architecture

NestJS modules by domain: Auth, Users, Workspaces, Invitations, Projects, Boards, Tasks, Comments, Realtime, Presence, Notifications, Activity, Files, Search. Controllers handle transport, services hold rules, Prisma repositories persist. Implemented: PrismaModule, HealthModule, AuthModule, UsersModule, WorkspacesModule, and ProjectsModule (including boards and columns). Health checks PostgreSQL and Redis. Shared bootstrap validation rejects missing foundation environment variables. Auth can return 503 when Supabase is not configured in local development.

## 11. Database Architecture

PostgreSQL is durable truth. Prisma schema and checked-in migrations define structure. Foreign keys, unique constraints, transactional writes, and indexed membership/path lookups are required. Initial migration introduced an infrastructure probe table; Phase 2 added `users` with unique `auth_subject`; Phase 3 added `workspaces`, `workspace_members`, and `workspace_invitations` with foreign keys, unique membership, and hashed invitation tokens. Phase 4 added `projects`, `boards`, and `columns` with lineage foreign keys and order indexes. Later domain tables arrive with their phases. Never edit production schema manually.

## 12. Data Models

Implemented: `User(id, authSubject, email, displayName, createdAt, updatedAt)` with unique Supabase subject; `Workspace(id, name, ownerId, archivedAt)`; `WorkspaceMember(id, workspaceId, userId, role, joinedAt)` with unique workspace/user; `WorkspaceInvitation(id, workspaceId, email, role, tokenHash, expiresAt, acceptedAt, revokedAt, invitedBy)`. Implemented Phase 4: `Project(id, workspaceId, name, description, status, createdBy, archivedAt)`, `Board(id, projectId, name, version)`, and `Column(id, boardId, name, position)`. All IDs UUID and timestamps UTC. Planned: `Task`, `TaskAssignee`, `Label`, `TaskLabel`, `Comment`, `Attachment`, `TaskWatcher`, `ActivityEvent`, and `Notification`. Model details are refined with migrations when each feature is implemented.

## 13. Authentication Architecture

Phase 2 implementation: Supabase Auth email/password and Google OAuth via PKCE callback. SSR stores session in cookies. Next.js `getClaims()` verifies before rendering `/app`; middleware refreshes the session and returns cache headers. The browser/server forwards the access token to NestJS. API independently verifies ES256/RS256 JWT signature against the configured project's JWKS, issuer `${SUPABASE_URL}/auth/v1`, audience `authenticated`, expiry, UUID subject, role, email, and non-anonymous claim. The verified subject maps to a local `User`, created on first protected profile access. Neither cookie content nor client-supplied IDs authorize the API. Socket handshake will reuse verification in Phase 6. The provider URL/publishable key and Google provider must be configured externally; no service role key is used.

## 14. Authorization / RBAC

Roles: OWNER (settings, membership roles, transfer, archive); ADMIN (rename, invite, projects/boards/columns); EDITOR (later tasks/comments/files); VIEWER (read only). Phase 3 API checks membership on each workspace path and hides inaccessible workspaces with 404. OWNER cannot be removed/demoted through the role route; transfer changes `ownerId` and both memberships in a serializable transaction. Admin cannot invite admin; no invitation can grant owner. Phase 4 project and board requests resolve workspace lineage server-side and enforce the same membership rules. Future nested resources must do the same. Socket room joins will require authorization in Phase 6.

## 15. REST API Design

Version prefix `/api/v1`. Implemented: `GET /health`; `GET/PATCH /users/me`; `GET/POST /workspaces`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /workspaces/:id` (DELETE archives), `GET /workspaces/:id/members`, `PATCH/DELETE /workspaces/:id/members/:memberId`, `DELETE /workspaces/:id/members/me`, `POST /workspaces/:id/transfer-ownership`, `GET/POST /workspaces/:id/invitations`, `DELETE /workspaces/:id/invitations/:invitationId`, `POST /invitations/accept`. Invitation create returns a raw token only once and uses `Cache-Control: private, no-store`; there is no email sender yet. Implemented Phase 4: `GET/POST /workspaces/:workspaceId/projects`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /projects/:projectId`, `GET/POST /projects/:projectId/boards`, `GET /boards/:boardId`, and board column add/rename/delete/order routes. Planned: task/comment/activity/notification/file/search endpoints. Mutations return canonical state. Validation errors 400, unauthenticated 401, unauthorized 403, absent 404, conflicts 409.

## 16. Real-Time Architecture

Socket.IO gateway will authorize handshake and joins. Rooms: `user:{id}`, `workspace:{id}`, `project:{id}`, `board:{id}`, `task:{id}`. Server sends events only after durable commit; outbox or reliable publication may be added when actual scale warrants it. Clients deduplicate event IDs and refetch on reconnect. Redis adapter distributes socket traffic across API instances in Phase 16.

## 17. WebSocket Event Catalog

Planned: `task.created`, `task.updated`, `task.moved`, `task.deleted`, `comment.created`, `comment.updated`, `comment.deleted`, `member.joined`, `member.left`, `presence.online`, `presence.offline`, `typing.started`, `typing.stopped`, `board.updated`, `notification.created`. Envelope: `{event,eventId,workspaceId,projectId?,boardId?,entityId,actorId,version?,timestamp,payload}`. The server constructs actor and routing fields; payloads should be small. No events exist in Phase 1.

## 18. Presence System

Redis TTL-backed per-user socket sets and heartbeat, scoped to workspaces. Online means at least one valid socket remains. On disconnect remove only that socket; expiry handles crashes. Reauthorize after reconnect. Presence never becomes permanent PostgreSQL truth.

## 19. Concurrency Strategy

Boards use integer `version` for column changes. Each column mutation sends `expectedVersion`; the server claims the matching board version in a serializable transaction and returns 409 on stale state. Reordering validates the exact column set and rewrites at most 20 positions. Tasks will use their own integer `version` and lexicographic rank for moves without rewriting a whole column; rebalance under transaction when tokens exhaust space.

## 20. Optimistic UI Strategy

For task moves, snapshot cache, apply immediate change, send expected version, reconcile returned canonical state, and roll back with an understandable conflict message on rejection. Invalidate/refetch after socket events or reconnection. Never silently overwrite an edit.

## 21. Redis Architecture

Phase 1 connection check proves configuration. Later use ephemeral presence, typing TTL, rate-limit state, and Socket.IO Redis adapter. PostgreSQL remains business truth. Redis outage should fail dependent readiness and trigger clear operational logs; product behavior will be specified per feature.

## 22. File Storage Architecture

Phase 12: private object bucket with signed upload/download URLs, workspace authorization, MIME/size validation, metadata in PostgreSQL, and explicit deletion handling. Never expose bucket-wide credentials.

## 23. Notification Architecture

Types: TASK_ASSIGNED, MENTION, COMMENT, DUE_DATE, INVITATION, ROLE_CHANGED. Durable per-user records with `readAt`, actor, entity, payload. Real-time event updates the UI count. Recipient authorization and deduplication are required.

## 24. Search Architecture

Start with indexed PostgreSQL full-text/filter queries scoped by workspace membership. Paginate results. Add dedicated search infrastructure only with measured need.

## 25. Security Requirements

Validate all API inputs; verify auth server-side; enforce RBAC on REST and sockets; escape/render comments safely; hash invitation tokens; rate limit sensitive routes; use secure headers and least-privilege secrets. Bound upload size and type. Audit privileged actions. Do not leak membership through errors or events. Production CORS must be explicit.

## 26. Performance Requirements

Avoid N+1 reads, whole-board broadcasts for tiny updates, and large socket payloads. Paginate comments, activity, notifications, and search. Add indexes for foreign keys and common filters. Measure latency before optimization.

## 27. Observability

Phase 1 uses Nest's built-in console logs and health checks for PostgreSQL/Redis. Future stages add structured request logs, request IDs, error tracking, metrics, and dashboards. Logs must not include tokens or user content by default.

## 28. Testing Strategy

Unit: permissions, versioning, ranking, invitation validation, event construction, presence calculations. Integration: DB, auth, membership, REST, conflicts, socket authorization/delivery. Playwright E2E: signup→workspace→project→task; invite acceptance; two-client move; viewer rejection; concurrent conflict. Seed only explicitly fake users. Current tests validate bootstrap/health, governance, JWT rejection/acceptance using local signed tokens and JWKS, profile persistence, workspace RBAC, invitation token handling, ownership transfer, archive, project/board ancestry and roles, column version conflicts and ordering, and safe redirect continuation. Live Supabase and Google OAuth E2E requires external configuration and remains untested.

## 29. CI/CD

GitHub Actions checks each non-merge commit for `process.md`, then installs dependencies, runs format, lint, typecheck, unit and integration tests, and builds. Postgres and Redis service containers support integration. Deployment and production release gates arrive in Phase 15.

## 30. Environment Variables

`DATABASE_URL` PostgreSQL connection; `REDIS_URL` Redis connection; `API_PORT` API listener; `WEB_ORIGIN` allowed browser origin; `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` API base used by Next.js; `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` browser project URL; `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` browser-safe key; `SUPABASE_URL` exact trusted API project URL. `STORAGE_BUCKET` is reserved for Phase 12. `.env.example` contains names and local sample values, never live secrets.

## 31. Local Development

Node 20+, pnpm 10+, Docker. `pnpm install`, `docker compose up -d`, `pnpm db:migrate`, `pnpm dev`. Web on 3000 and API on 3001. `pnpm check`, `pnpm test:integration`, `pnpm build` validate the foundation. No seed command exists until domain data models are added.

## 32. Deployment Architecture

Planned: separate web and API services, managed PostgreSQL/Redis, private object storage. Terminate TLS at ingress; isolate DB/Redis networks; use managed secret injection, migrations before API rollout, rollback procedures, and backups. No production deployment exists yet. For multiple Socket.IO instances, document transport/sticky-session settings and shared Redis adapter in Phase 16.

## 33. Database Migration Rules

Check in schema and migration SQL. Generate migration locally, review destructive changes, test on a fresh DB, apply via `pnpm db:migrate` in CI/deployment. Never use schema push as a substitute for production migrations. Document data backfills and rollback plans.

## 34. Git Workflow

Focused branches and conventional commit subjects. Review staged changes, run relevant tests, update `process.md`, update this file if architecture changed, then stage/commit. Husky pre-commit and CI verify documentation. Merge commits may be exempt from per-commit process checks.

## 35. process.md Rules

Keep Current Status accurate. Every meaningful commit adds a dated `PROC-XXX` entry with summary, reason, affected modules, DB/API/socket/security effects, tests actually run, issues, decisions, current state, and next task. Never label planned functionality implemented.

## 36. Definition of Done

Implementation, strict typing, validation, server authorization, errors, relevant tests passing, usable loading/error/empty UI, real-time reconciliation where relevant, docs and process updated, and security review. A phase is complete only when its acceptance criteria run successfully.

## 37. Current Development Phase

Phase 4 — Projects, boards, and columns locally implemented and tested using mock JWKS. Live Supabase/Google flows need project configuration. Next is Phase 5 tasks. GitHub Actions has not run remotely. See `process.md` for exact results.

## 38. Complete Product Roadmap

Phase 0 documentation/governance; 1 monorepo/web/API/Postgres/Redis/health/env/logging; 2 Supabase auth and profile; 3 workspaces/membership/RBAC/invitations; 4 projects/boards/columns; 5 tasks, assignment, rank, drag/drop; 6 authenticated Socket.IO events; 7 multi-tab Redis presence; 8 optimistic UI and version conflicts; 9 comments, mentions, typing; 10 activity history; 11 notifications; 12 attachments; 13 scoped search; 14 security/performance/accessibility hardening; 15 deployment and operations; 16 multi-instance Socket.IO Redis adapter; 17 optional Yjs collaborative notes; 18 privacy-conscious aggregate analytics. MVP requires two users to create accounts, join a workspace, use the same board, create/move/assign/comment on tasks, see live updates/presence/activity, with RBAC, secure sockets, concurrency, tests, CI, and docs.

## 39. Known Issues / Technical Debt

Tasks, sockets, and other collaboration domains are not implemented. Project/board authenticated screens have not been visually checked against a live signed-in session. Workspace invitation delivery is a one-time share link; email sending is not implemented. Workspace archive has no restore UI/API yet. The DB probe is a temporary infrastructure aid. Supabase credentials and Google OAuth provider are absent, so live browser authentication is untested. CI has not run on GitHub and no production deployment exists. Future event delivery must specify recovery of missed publications before production use. Status is updated in `process.md` after checks.

## 40. Architecture Decision Log

### ADR-001 — NestJS backend
Status: Accepted. Context: modular API with guards and socket gateway needed. Decision: NestJS. Alternatives Considered: Express/Fastify alone. Consequences: conventions and dependency injection aid maintainability; more framework setup.

### ADR-002 — PostgreSQL and Prisma
Status: Accepted. Context: relational collaborative data and migrations. Decision: PostgreSQL plus Prisma with checked-in migrations. Alternatives Considered: document store, raw SQL. Consequences: transactional constraints and type-safe access; ORM version must be pinned and migrations reviewed.

### ADR-003 — Socket.IO and Redis
Status: Accepted for future phases. Context: authorized rooms, reconnection, multiple API instances. Decision: Socket.IO and Redis adapter/presence. Alternatives Considered: raw WebSocket and database polling. Consequences: extra infrastructure and protocol versioning; REST remains authoritative.

### ADR-004 — Task ranking and versioning
Status: Accepted for Phase 5/8 implementation. Context: moves and conflicting edits. Decision: lexicographic ranks plus compare-and-swap integer versions. Alternatives Considered: renumbering all positions, last-write-wins. Consequences: occasional rebalance transaction; explicit conflict UI.

### ADR-005 — Supabase Auth
Status: Accepted for Phase 2 implementation. Context: email/password and Google OAuth. Decision: Supabase-hosted identity with independent API token verification. Alternatives Considered: custom password store. Consequences: provider configuration and external service dependency.

## 41. Future Features

Yjs-backed collaborative notes, live cursors, shared selection, and aggregate project analytics follow a stable MVP. Avoid invasive individual productivity tracking.

## 42. New Developer Onboarding

Clone; read `AGENTS.md`, this file, and `process.md`; inspect status/tests; install pnpm dependencies; start Docker services; migrate; run dev/check. The current feature set and next task are always in `process.md`.

## 43. AI Coding Agent Instructions

Follow the same onboarding and commit process as humans. Inspect code and tests before edits. Distinguish plan from implementation. Resolve documentation drift. Preserve security boundaries and typed public contracts. Report real test outcomes and unresolved limits.
